SKOWHEGAN &#151; More than 30 Skowhegan Area High School students were
suspended from school Friday, a day after they held a sit-in on
campus, boycotting classes.

The protesters say they refused to attend class because they have been
subjected to harassment and intimidation by a group of students who
favor Gothic dress and call themselves "The Carnival Killers."

School officials said those claims are nothing more than rumor. The
self-described leader of the Carnival group called the allegations a
lie.

Principal Richard Wilson said the three-day suspensions stemmed from
insubordination because the students refused to attend classes. He
said Friday he did not know the exact number of suspended students
because he was still talking to some.

"Some kids chose to go back to class, and some kids dug their heels
in, and we've had to deal with them more strictly," Wilson said. "It's
all about the insubordination."

Suspended students, many of whom protested with signs during a hard
rain Friday morning, insisted it was more about harassment. One sign
read, "Say no to gangs."

Zack Field, a junior, said harassment has been a problem all year.
"They take up a section of the school (hall) and harass you when you
walk by and throw things like backpacks," he said.

Contacted on his day off, acting Police Chief Rick Bonneau said he has
received no complaints from students or parents.

Junior Megan Davis, who was not among the suspended students, said she
had heard rumors of potential violence. "I heard (members of The
Carnival Killers) were going to attack all the juniors," Davis said.
"They call the juniors smart, prissy and rich. They were throwing
doughnuts and spitting on people."

Wilson said the claims of threatened violence are all part of "the
rumor mill." Student safety has not been an issue and no weapons have
been seen, he said.

Justin Longevin, a senior from Canaan and self-described leader of The
Carnival Killers, said no weapons have been brought to school.

"All the rest is a lie," Longevin said. "Not once has any one of my
boys or girls threatened them. They're saying that because they don't
want to look like the bad guys."



